Day 1, explained 

Keshav’s method says: don’t read a paper start to finish. Read it in up to three rounds, and stop early if it isn’t useful.

Pass 1 (5–10 minutes): read the title, abstract, introduction, section headings, and conclusion. Afterwards you should know what problem the paper solves, what kind of paper it is (new method? experiment? survey?), and whether it’s relevant to you. If it’s not relevant, stop here. Most papers stop here.
Pass 2 (about 1 hour): read the whole paper but skip heavy math and proofs. Look carefully at figures and tables. Can you be able to explain the main idea to a friend.
Pass 3 (several hours): only for the few papers central to your research. You mentally “re-do” the work: question every assumption, ask how you’d do it yourself. This is where you find weaknesses, and weaknesses become research questions.
